    "candidate_text": "For each \\(x\\in[0,1)\\), \\(x^n\\to 0\\), while \\(f_n(1)=1\\) for every \\(n\\). Hence the pointwise limit is\n\\[\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&0\\le x<1,\\\\1,&x=1.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIf \\((f_n)\\) converged uniformly, its uniform limit would necessarily be \\(f\\). Set \\(\\varepsilon=\\tfrac12\\). For every \\(N\\in\\mathbb Z_+\\), choose \\(n=N\\) and \\(x=2^{-1/N}\\in[0,1)\\). Then \\(f(x)=0\\) and\n\\[\n|f_n(x)-f(x)|=x^N=\\frac12.\n\\]\nThus, for every \\(N\\), there exist \\(n\\ge N\\) and \\(x\\in[0,1]\\) such that \\(|f_n(x)-f(x)|\\ge\\varepsilon\\), contradicting the definition of uniform convergence. Therefore \\((f_n)\\) does not converge uniformly on \\([0,1]\\).",
